## 2️⃣ Placement Groups (PGs) – Configuration

### View PG usage and states

```bash
ceph pg stat                 # High-level PG state summary
ceph pg dump | head          # Low-level PG info (advanced)
```

### PG sizing rule of thumb

```text
~100–200 PGs per OSD
```

### Example: Set PGs for a pool (increase gradually)

```bash
ceph osd pool set rbd-pool pg_num 256     # Total PGs in pool
ceph osd pool set rbd-pool pgp_num 256    # PGs used for placement
```

---

## 3️⃣ Erasure Coding (EC)

### Create an EC profile (k=data, m=parity)

```bash
ceph osd erasure-code-profile set ec-profile \
  k=4 m=2 crush-failure-domain=host        # Can lose 2 hosts
```

### Create an EC pool

```bash
ceph osd pool create ec-pool 128 128 erasure ec-profile
```

> ⚠️ Do NOT use EC for RBD / VM workloads (latency & RMW penalty).

---

## 4️⃣ CRUSH Rules & Failure Domains

### View CRUSH hierarchy

```bash
ceph osd tree                # root → rack → host → osd
```

### Create a replicated CRUSH rule (failure domain = host)

```bash
ceph osd crush rule create-replicated rbd-rule default host
```

### Apply CRUSH rule to a pool

```bash
ceph osd pool set rbd-pool crush_rule rbd-rule
```

---

## 5️⃣ Reweighting & Balancing

### Manually reduce data on a specific OSD

```bash
ceph osd reweight osd.3 0.8  # Temporarily move data away
```

### Automatically balance based on utilization

```bash
ceph osd reweight-by-utilization   # Safe for mixed disks
```

### View OSD capacity and usage

```bash
ceph osd df                 # Per-OSD space usage
```

---

## 6️⃣ Monitoring & Orchestration (cephadm)

```bash
ceph orch ps                # All running Ceph daemons
ceph orch host ls            # Hosts known to Ceph
```

---

## 7️⃣ Emergency / Must‑Know Commands

```bash
ceph -s                      # First command in any incident
ceph health detail           # Why cluster is unhappy
ceph osd stat                # OSD up/in counts
```

---

### Production Wisdom (Read Once)

* Never remove a node before PGs are **active+clean**
* RBD → Replication, RGW/Backup → Erasure Coding
* Wrong PG count causes more outages than disk failures
* CRUSH failure-domain should almost always be **host**

